, the authors embarked on the first computer exploration of Klein's vision, and in doing so found many further extraordinary images. Join the authors on the path from basic mathematical ideas to the simple algorithms that create the delicate fractal filigrees, most of which have never appeared in print before. David Wright is Professor at Oklahoma State University. He has held a guest professorship at the University of Göttingen, and was Sloan Fellow at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton from 1988 to 1990. From 1997 to 1999 he helped establish the famous William Lowell Putnam Mathematical Competition. In 2009 he received a Distinguished Teaching Award from the Mathematical Association of America.\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Indra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix Klein\nIndra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix Klein, Caroline Series\nProfessional (Chartered Institute of Marketing)\nAuthors  | David Mumford, Caroline Series, David James Wright, David Wright\nEdition  | illustrated, reprint\nPublisher  | Cambridge University Press, 2002\nISBN  | 0521352533, 9780521352536\nLength  | 395 pages\nSubjects  | Computers › Software Development & Engineering › Computer Graphics\nComputers / Software Development & Engineering / Computer Graphics\nMathematics / Algebra / General\nMathematics / Discrete Mathematics\nMathematics / General\nMathematics / Geometry / General\nMathematics / Group Theory\nMathematics / History & Philosophy\nMathematics / Topology--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndra's Pearls: The Vision of Felix Klein - David Mumford, Caroline Series, David James Wright, David Wright - Google Books (https://books.google.co.in/books?id=XFE3jmSEfC8C)\nciteturn827search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; David Mumford, Caroline Series, David James Wright, David Wright Cambridge University Press, Apr 25, 2002 - Computers - 395 pages Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth