Caroline Series, David James Wright, David Wright\nEdition  | illustrated, reprint\nPublisher  | Cambridge University Press, 2002\nISBN  | 0521352533, 9780521352536\nLength  | 395 pages\nSubjects  | Computers › Software Development & Engineering › Computer Graphics\nComputers / Software Development & Engineering / Computer Graphics\nMathematics / Algebra / General\nMathematics / Discrete Mathematics\nMathematics / General\nMathematics / Geometry / General\nMathematics / Group Theory\nMathematics / History & Philosophy\nMathematics / Topology--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein | WorldCat.org (https://search.worldcat.org/title/Indra%27s-pearls-%3A-the-vision-of-Felix-Klein/oclc/49859120)\nciteturn827search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Image: Front cover image for Indra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein ... David Mumford, Caroline Series, David James Wright ... Publisher: Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2002\n\nImage: Front cover image for Indra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein\n\n# \n\nIndra's pearls : the vision of Felix Klein\n\nAuthors:\n\nDavid Mumford, Caroline Series, David James Wright\n\nSummary:\"Felix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl. Klein studied infinitely repeated reflections and was led to forms with multiple co-existing symmetries, each simple in itself, but whose interactions produce fractals on the edge of chaos. For a century these images, which were practically impossible to draw by hand, barely existed outside the imagination of mathematicians. However in the 1980s the authors embarked on the first computer exploration of Klein's vision, and in so doing found further extraordinary images of their own. Join the authors on the path from some basic mathemathical ideas to the simple algorithms that create the delicate fractal filigrees, most of which have never appeared in print before. Beginners can learn to understand what the images mean and follow the step-by-step instructions for writing computer programs that generate them. More advanced readers can see how the images relate to ideas that take them to the forefront of research.\"--Jacket\n\n* * *\n\nPrint Book, English, 2002\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2002--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[PDF] Indra's Pearls by David Mumford | 9780521352536, 9780511058974 (https://www.perlego.com/book/4219138/indras-pearls-the-vision-of-felix-klein-pdf)\nciteturn827search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Image: Indra's PearlseBook - PDF ... David Mumford, ... Cambridge University Press ... 2002 ... 9780521352536\n\nImage: Indra's Pearls\n\neBook - PDF\n\n# Indra's Pearls\n\n## The Vision of Felix Klein\n\nDavid Mumford,\n\nCaroline Series,\n\nDavid Wright,\n\n# Indra's Pearls\n\n## The Vision of Felix Klein\n\nDavid Mumford,\n\nCaroline Series,\n\nDavid Wright,\n\nBook details Table of contents Citations\n\n## About this book\n\nFelix Klein, one of the great nineteenth-century geometers, discovered in mathematics an idea prefigured in Buddhist mythology: the heaven of Indra contained a net of pearls, each of which was reflected in its neighbour, so that the whole Universe was mirrored in each pearl. Klein studied infinitely repeated reflections and was led to forms with multiple coexisting symmetries. For a century, these images barely existed outside the imagination of mathematicians. However, in the 1980s, the authors embarked on the first computer exploration of Klein's vision, and in doing so found many further extraordinary images. Join the authors on the path from basic mathematical ideas to the simple algorithms th